 "best_diff": "--- initial\n+++ best\n@@ -19,7 +19,33 @@\n     features (they are standardized and a bias is added), so nonlinear structure only\n     helps if a feature expresses it. Only the Python standard library may be used.\n \n-    Current method: the raw inputs.\n+    Method: Include raw inputs, pairwise products, and their signs.\n     \"\"\"\n-    return list(row)\n+    x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6 = row\n+    features_list = [x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6]\n+    \n+    # Pairwise interactions\n+    features_list.extend([x1 * x2, x1 * x3, x1 * x6,\n+                            x2 * x3, x2 * x6,\n+                            x3 * x4, x3 * x5, x4 * x5,\n+                            x4 * x6, x5 * x6])\n+    \n+    # Binning for x3 (lognormal-like)\n+    # Define bins for x3, ensuring x3 > 0 is handled. We use 3 bins.\n+    x3_bins = [1.0, 5.0]\n+    \n+    if x3 >= x3_bins[1]:\n+        features_list.append(1.0)\n+    elif x3 >= x3_bins[0]:\n+        features_list.append(1.0)\n+    else:\n+        features_list.append(0.0)\n+        \n+    # Threshold features based on uniform inputs x4 and x5, using a sweep approach.\n+    thresholds = [0.5, 1.0, 1.5]\n+    for t in thresholds:\n+        features_list.append(1.0 if x4 > t else 0.0)\n+        features_list.append(1.0 if x5 > t else 0.0)\n+        \n+    return features_list\n # OMEGA-BLOCK-END\n",
